                'body'=>"The 4-7-8 breathing technique, also known as \"relaxing breath,\" was developed by Dr. Andrew Weil. It acts as a natural tranquilizer for the nervous system.\n\nHow to Practice:\n\n1. Find a comfortable seated position and relax your shoulders.\n2. Place the tip of your tongue against the ridge behind your upper front teeth.\n3. Exhale completely through your mouth, making a whoosh sound.\n4. Close your mouth and inhale quietly through your nose for 4 seconds.\n5. Hold your breath for 7 seconds.\n6. Exhale completely through your mouth for 8 seconds, making a whoosh sound.\n7. This completes one cycle. Repeat 3-4 times.\n\nWhen to Use It:\n- Before sleep to combat insomnia\n- During moments of anxiety or panic\n- Before a stressful event (exam, presentation, difficult conversation)\n- Anytime you need to calm your nervous system\n\nTips for Success:\n- Practice twice daily for best results\n- Start with 4 cycles and gradually increase\n- If holding for 7 seconds is difficult, speed up but keep the 4:7:8 ratio\n- The exhale should always be twice as long as the inhale\n\nWhy It Works:\nThis technique forces your mind to focus on breathing rather than worries. The extended exhale activates your parasympathetic nervous system (your body's 'rest and digest' mode), lowering heart rate and blood pressure."
            ],
            [
                'title'=>'Progressive Muscle Relaxation','description'=>'Systematically tense and release muscle groups to reduce physical tension and stress.','category'=>'stress','content_type'=>'exercise','tags'=>['relaxation','stress','body'],'icon'=>'💪','duration'=>'15 minutes','difficulty'=>'beginner',
                'body'=>"Progressive Muscle Relaxation (PMR) was developed by Edmund Jacobson in the 1930s. It involves tensing and then releasing different muscle groups to achieve deep physical relaxation.\n\nStep-by-Step Guide:\n\n1. Find a quiet, comfortable place. Lie down or sit comfortably.\n2. Close your eyes and take 3 deep breaths.\n3. Start with your feet:\n   - Curl your toes tightly for 5 seconds\n   - Release and notice the relaxation for 10 seconds\n4. Move to your calves:\n   - Point your toes toward your shins for 5 seconds\n   - Release and relax for 10 seconds\n5. Continue upward through each muscle group:\n   - Thighs (press knees together)\n   - Stomach (tighten abdominal muscles)\n   - Chest (take a deep breath and hold)\n   - Hands (make tight fists)\n   - Arms (curl biceps)\n   - Shoulders (shrug toward ears)\n   - Face (scrunch all facial muscles)\n6. Finally, tense your entire body at once for 5 seconds, then release.\n\nKey Principles:\n- Never tense to the point of pain\n- Focus on the contrast between tension and relaxation\n- The relaxation phase is more important than the tension phase\n- Practice daily for cumulative benefits\n\nBenefits:\n- Reduces muscle tension and physical stress\n- Improves sleep quality\n- Lowers blood pressure\n- Decreases anxiety and panic symptoms\n- Helps with headaches and chronic pain"
            ],
            [
                'title'=>'Understanding Depression','description'=>'Learn about depression symptoms, causes, and evidence-based treatment options.','category'=>'depression','content_type'=>'article','tags'=>['depression','education','mental health'],'icon'=>'📖','duration'=>'8 min read','difficulty'=>'beginner',
                'body'=>"Depression is more than just feeling sad. It is a serious but treatable medical condition that affects how you feel, think, and handle daily activities.\n\nCommon Symptoms:\n- Persistent sad, anxious, or \"empty\" mood\n- Loss of interest in activities you once enjoyed\n- Changes in appetite or weight\n- Difficulty sleeping or oversleeping\n- Loss of energy or increased fatigue\n- Feelings of worthlessness or guilt\n- Difficulty concentrating or making decisions\n- Thoughts of death or suicide\n\nWhat Causes Depression:\nDepression has no single cause. It often results from a combination of:\n- Biological factors (brain chemistry, hormones, genetics)\n- Psychological factors (negative thinking patterns, trauma)\n- Environmental factors (stress, loss, isolation)\n\nEvidence-Based Treatments:\n\n1. Psychotherapy\n   - Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT): Helps identify and change negative thought patterns\n   - Interpersonal Therapy (IPT): Focuses on improving relationships\n   - Behavioral Activation: Gradually re-engaging in meaningful activities\n\n2. Lifestyle Changes\n   - Regular exercise (30 minutes, 5 times a week)\n   - Consistent sleep schedule\n   - Balanced nutrition\n   - Social connection\n   - Limiting alcohol and avoiding drugs\n\n3. Professional Help\n   - Consult your primary care doctor\n   - See a licensed therapist or counselor\n   - Consider psychiatric evaluation if symptoms are severe\n\nRemember:\n- Depression is NOT a sign of weakness\n- Recovery is possible with the right support\n- Reaching out for help is a sign of strength\n- If you are in crisis, contact a helpline immediately"
            ],
            [
                'title'=>'Sleep Hygiene Guide','description'=>'Practical tips for improving your sleep quality and establishing a healthy bedtime routine.','category'=>'sleep','content_type'=>'article','tags'=>['sleep','habits','wellness'],'icon'=>'🌙','duration'=>'6 min read','difficulty'=>'beginner',
                'body'=>"Good sleep hygiene means having habits that help you get quality sleep consistently. Poor sleep affects mood, concentration, immunity, and mental health.\n\nThe Golden Rules of Sleep Hygiene:\n\n1. Stick to a Schedule\n   - Go to bed and wake up at the same time daily, even on weekends\n   - Your body's internal clock thrives on consistency\n\n2. Create a Sleep-Friendly Environment\n   - Keep your bedroom cool (18-20°C / 65-68°F)\n   - Make it dark: use blackout curtains or an eye mask\n   - Keep it quiet: use earplugs or a white noise machine\n   - Reserve your bed for sleep only (no work, no scrolling)\n\n3. Watch What You Consume\n   - Avoid caffeine after 2 PM\n   - Limit alcohol (it fragments sleep)\n   - Don't eat heavy meals within 2-3 hours of bedtime\n   - Stay hydrated but don't drink too much before bed\n\n4. Build a Wind-Down Routine (30-60 minutes before bed)\n   - Dim the lights\n   - Put away screens (blue light blocks melatonin)\n   - Try gentle stretching, reading, or a warm bath\n   - Practice relaxation techniques\n\n5. Daytime Habits That Improve Night Sleep\n   - Get natural sunlight within 1 hour of waking\n   - Exercise regularly (but not within 3 hours of bed)\n   - Limit naps to 20 minutes before 3 PM\n\nIf You Can't Fall Asleep:\n- Don't watch the clock\n- If you're awake for 20+ minutes, get up and do something calm\n- Return to bed only when you feel sleepy\n- Avoid screens during this time"
            ],
            [
                'title'=>'5-Minute Mindfulness Meditation','description'=>'A quick guided meditation to center yourself and reduce stress.','category'=>'meditation','content_type'=>'exercise','tags'=>['meditation','mindfulness','calm'],'icon'=>'🧘','duration'=>'5 minutes','difficulty'=>'beginner',
                'body'=>"This simple meditation can be done anywhere — at your desk, on a park bench, or in bed. All you need is 5 minutes.\n\nGuided Practice:\n\nMinute 1 — Arrive\n- Sit comfortably with your feet flat on the floor\n- Rest your hands on your lap\n- Gently close your eyes or soften your gaze\n- Take 3 deep breaths: in through the nose, out through the mouth\n\nMinute 2 — Notice Your Body\n- Scan from the top of your head to your toes\n- Notice any areas of tension without trying to change them\n- Let your breathing return to its natural rhythm\n\nMinute 3 — Follow Your Breath\n- Focus your attention on the sensation of breathing\n- Notice the air entering your nostrils, your chest rising, your belly expanding\n- When your mind wanders (and it will), gently bring attention back to your breath\n- No judgment — wandering minds are completely normal\n\nMinute 4 — Expand Awareness\n- While maintaining awareness of your breath, expand attention outward\n- Notice sounds around you without labeling them as good or bad\n- Notice the temperature of the air on your skin\n- Simply observe without reacting\n\nMinute 5 — Return\n- Bring your attention back to your body sitting in the chair\n- Wiggle your fingers and toes\n- Take one final deep breath\n- Open your eyes slowly\n\nTips:\n- Set a gentle timer so you don't worry about time\n- Don't aim for a \"blank mind\" — that's a myth\n- Consistency matters more than duration\n- Try to practice at the same time each day"
