            [
                'title'=>'Managing Exam Stress','description'=>'Strategies for students to cope with academic pressure and perform their best.','category'=>'stress','content_type'=>'article','tags'=>['students','exams','stress'],'icon'=>'📚','duration'=>'7 min read','difficulty'=>'beginner',
                'body'=>"Exam stress is one of the most common challenges students face. Some stress is normal and can even help performance, but too much can be paralyzing.\n\nBefore Exams — Preparation Strategies:\n\n1. Plan Your Study Schedule\n   - Break material into manageable chunks\n   - Use spaced repetition (study over multiple days, not all at once)\n   - Prioritize topics by difficulty and importance\n   - Include breaks every 25-50 minutes (Pomodoro Technique)\n\n2. Active Study Methods\n   - Teach concepts to someone else (or explain aloud to yourself)\n   - Create mind maps and visual summaries\n   - Practice with past papers under timed conditions\n   - Use flashcards for key terms and concepts\n\n3. Take Care of Your Body\n   - Sleep 7-9 hours (sleep consolidates memory)\n   - Eat regular, balanced meals\n   - Exercise daily, even if just a 20-minute walk\n   - Stay hydrated\n\nDuring Exams — Performance Tips:\n\n1. Read all questions before starting\n2. Start with questions you feel most confident about\n3. If you feel panicked, use box breathing (4-4-4-4)\n4. Don't compare your progress with others\n5. If you go blank, skip and return later\n\nAfter Exams — Recovery:\n- Avoid post-mortems with classmates\n- Do something enjoyable and relaxing\n- Remember: one exam does not define your worth\n- If stress persists, talk to a counselor\n\nRemember:\n- Perfection is not the goal — doing your best is enough\n- Struggling does not mean you are not smart enough\n- It is okay to ask for help or extensions when overwhelmed"
            ],
            [
                'title'=>'Box Breathing Exercise','description'=>'A calming technique used by Navy SEALs: breathe in, hold, out, hold — each for 4 seconds.','category'=>'anxiety','content_type'=>'breathing','tags'=>['breathing','focus','calm'],'icon'=>'📦','duration'=>'4 minutes','difficulty'=>'beginner',
                'body'=>"Box breathing (also called square breathing) is used by Navy SEALs, athletes, and first responders to stay calm under extreme pressure. It's simple, effective, and can be done anywhere.\n\nHow to Practice:\n\n1. Sit upright in a comfortable position\n2. Slowly exhale all the air from your lungs\n3. Breathe IN through your nose for 4 seconds\n4. HOLD your breath for 4 seconds\n5. Breathe OUT through your mouth for 4 seconds\n6. HOLD (lungs empty) for 4 seconds\n7. Repeat for 4-5 cycles\n\nVisualize a Box:\nImagine tracing a square as you breathe:\n- Left side going up = Inhale (4 seconds)\n- Top side going right = Hold (4 seconds)\n- Right side going down = Exhale (4 seconds)\n- Bottom side going left = Hold (4 seconds)\n\nWhen to Use Box Breathing:\n- Before a stressful meeting or presentation\n- When anxiety begins to build\n- During or after an argument\n- Before making an important decision\n- When you need to regain focus\n- Before sleep\n\nWhy It Works:\n- Activates the parasympathetic nervous system\n- Lowers cortisol (stress hormone) levels\n- Improves focus and mental clarity\n- Regulates the autonomic nervous system\n\nAdvanced Variation:\nOnce comfortable with 4 seconds, try increasing to 5 or 6 seconds per phase. The longer the cycle, the deeper the relaxation response."
            ],
            [
                'title'=>'Recognizing Burnout','description'=>'How to identify the signs of burnout before it becomes overwhelming.','category'=>'burnout','content_type'=>'article','tags'=>['burnout','work','self-care'],'icon'=>'🔥','duration'=>'6 min read','difficulty'=>'beginner',
                'body'=>"Burnout is not just being tired — it is a state of chronic physical and emotional exhaustion caused by prolonged stress. Recognizing it early is crucial for recovery.\n\nThe Three Dimensions of Burnout:\n\n1. Exhaustion\n   - Feeling drained physically and emotionally\n   - Difficulty getting out of bed\n   - Constant fatigue that rest does not fix\n\n2. Cynicism / Detachment\n   - Feeling disconnected from your work or relationships\n   - Increased irritability and negativity\n   - Loss of purpose or meaning\n\n3. Reduced Effectiveness\n   - Decreased productivity despite working harder\n   - Difficulty concentrating\n   - Feeling incompetent or like a failure\n\nEarly Warning Signs:\n- Dreading work or responsibilities\n- Withdrawing from social interactions\n- Neglecting personal needs (sleep, exercise, nutrition)\n- Physical symptoms (headaches, stomach issues, frequent illness)\n- Using substances to cope\n- Feeling trapped or hopeless\n\nRecovery Strategies:\n\n1. Set Boundaries\n   - Learn to say no to additional commitments\n   - Set work hours and stick to them\n   - Turn off work notifications after hours\n\n2. Prioritize Recovery\n   - Schedule non-negotiable rest time\n   - Engage in activities that recharge you\n   - Take actual breaks (not working through lunch)\n\n3. Seek Support\n   - Talk to your manager about workload\n   - Connect with supportive friends and family\n   - Consider professional counseling\n\n4. Reassess\n   - Evaluate your values and whether your current path aligns\n   - Consider what changes are within your control\n   - Small changes can make a significant difference"
            ],
            [
                'title'=>'Gratitude Journaling Guide','description'=>'How keeping a gratitude journal can improve your mental health and overall wellbeing.','category'=>'meditation','content_type'=>'article','tags'=>['gratitude','journaling','positivity'],'icon'=>'📝','duration'=>'5 min read','difficulty'=>'beginner',
                'body'=>"Research shows that regularly practicing gratitude can increase happiness by 25%, improve sleep quality, and reduce symptoms of depression.\n\nHow to Start a Gratitude Journal:\n\n1. Choose Your Format\n   - A physical notebook (many people find handwriting more impactful)\n   - A digital journal or app (like the journal feature on this platform)\n   - Even a simple notes app on your phone\n\n2. Set a Daily Time\n   - Morning: Sets a positive tone for the day\n   - Evening: Helps you reflect and sleep better\n   - Pick one time and be consistent\n\n3. The Practice (5 minutes)\n   - Write down 3-5 things you are grateful for\n   - Be specific (not just \"family\" but \"my sister's encouraging text this morning\")\n   - Include small things (a good cup of coffee, sunshine, a stranger's smile)\n   - Try to find new things each day\n\nPrompts to Get Started:\n- What made me smile today?\n- Who am I grateful for and why?\n- What is something I often take for granted?\n- What challenge taught me something valuable?\n- What simple pleasure did I enjoy today?\n\nDeepening Your Practice:\n- Write why each item matters to you\n- Include at least one thing about yourself you appreciate\n- Revisit past entries when you are feeling low\n- Share your gratitude with others\n\nThe Science:\n- Gratitude activates the brain's reward system\n- It reduces the stress hormone cortisol by 23%\n- Regular practice physically changes brain structure over time\n- It builds resilience against depression and anxiety"
            ],
            [
                'title'=>'Body Scan Meditation','description'=>'A guided body scan to release tension and increase body awareness.','category'=>'meditation','content_type'=>'exercise','tags'=>['meditation','body','relaxation'],'icon'=>'🧘','duration'=>'10 minutes','difficulty'=>'beginner',
                'body'=>"A body scan meditation systematically brings awareness to every part of your body, helping release stored tension and promoting deep relaxation.\n\nPreparation:\n- Lie down on your back on a comfortable surface\n- Arms resting at your sides, palms up\n- Close your eyes\n- Take 3 deep breaths\n\nThe Practice:\n\n1. Feet (1 minute)\n   Bring attention to the soles of your feet. Notice any sensations — warmth, tingling, pressure. Breathe into this area and release.\n\n2. Legs (1.5 minutes)\n   Move awareness up through your ankles, calves, knees, and thighs. Notice any tightness. With each exhale, imagine tension melting away.\n\n3. Pelvis and Lower Back (1 minute)\n   Notice the weight of your hips against the surface. Breathe into your lower back, a common area for stored stress.\n\n4. Abdomen and Chest (1.5 minutes)\n   Feel your belly rising and falling with each breath. Notice your heartbeat. Allow your chest to soften with each exhale.\n\n5. Hands and Arms (1 minute)\n   Bring awareness to your fingertips, palms, wrists, forearms, and upper arms. Let any tension dissolve.\n\n6. Shoulders and Neck (1.5 minutes)\n   These hold the most tension for many people. Breathe deeply into your shoulders. With each exhale, let them drop away from your ears.\n\n7. Face and Head (1.5 minutes)\n   Soften your jaw. Unclench your teeth. Relax your forehead, the muscles around your eyes, and your scalp.\n\n8. Whole Body (1 minute)\n   Expand awareness to your entire body at once. Feel yourself as one unified whole, relaxed and present. Rest here for a few breaths.\n\nClosing:\n   Gently wiggle your fingers and toes. Take a deep breath. Open your eyes slowly when you are ready."
            ],
            [
                'title'=>'Healthy Relationship Boundaries','description'=>'Learn to set and maintain healthy boundaries in your relationships.','category'=>'relationships','content_type'=>'article','tags'=>['relationships','boundaries','communication'],'icon'=>'💛','duration'=>'7 min read','difficulty'=>'intermediate',
                'body'=>"Boundaries are the limits we set in relationships that allow us to protect our wellbeing while maintaining healthy connections with others.\n\nTypes of Boundaries:\n\n1. Physical Boundaries\n   - Personal space and touch preferences\n   - Privacy needs\n   - Physical needs (sleep, food, rest)\n\n2. Emotional Boundaries\n   - Separating your feelings from others'\n   - Not taking responsibility for others' emotions\n   - Protecting yourself from emotional manipulation\n\n3. Time Boundaries\n   - How you spend your time\n   - Saying no to commitments that drain you\n   - Protecting time for self-care\n\n4. Digital Boundaries\n   - Response time expectations\n   - Social media sharing limits\n   - Privacy online\n\nHow to Set Boundaries:\n\n1. Identify Your Needs\n   - What makes you feel uncomfortable or resentful?\n   - What do you need to feel safe and respected?\n\n2. Communicate Clearly\n   - Use \"I\" statements: \"I need...\" instead of \"You always...\"\n   - Be specific: \"I need 30 minutes alone after work\" vs. \"I need space\"\n   - Be firm but kind\n\n3. Follow Through\n   - Boundaries without consequences are just suggestions\n   - State what you will do if the boundary is crossed\n   - Actually follow through\n\n4. Handle Pushback\n   - Some people will resist your new boundaries\n   - This does not mean your boundary is wrong\n   - Repeat calmly: \"I understand, but this is what I need\"\n\nSigns of Unhealthy Boundaries:\n- Saying yes when you want to say no\n- Feeling responsible for others' happiness\n- Allowing others to define your feelings\n- Giving more than you can afford (time, energy, money)\n- Tolerating disrespect or abuse\n\nRemember: Setting boundaries is not selfish — it is self-care."
            ],
            [
                'title'=>'Work-Life Balance Tips','description'=>'Practical strategies for maintaining balance between professional and personal life.','category'=>'burnout','content_type'=>'article','tags'=>['work','balance','wellness'],'icon'=>'⚖️','duration'=>'6 min read','difficulty'=>'intermediate',
                'body'=>"Work-life balance does not mean perfect 50/50 — it means creating a sustainable rhythm where both work and personal life receive adequate attention.\n\nStrategies That Actually Work:\n\n1. Define Your Non-Negotiables\n   - Identify 3-5 activities that are essential for your wellbeing\n   - Schedule them like important meetings\n   - Examples: family dinner, exercise, 8 hours of sleep, a hobby\n\n2. Set Work Boundaries\n   - Define clear start and end times for work\n   - Create a shutdown ritual (close laptop, change clothes, take a walk)\n   - Turn off work notifications outside work hours\n   - If working from home, have a dedicated workspace\n\n3. Learn to Prioritize\n   - Use the Eisenhower Matrix: Urgent/Important vs. Not Urgent/Not Important\n   - Accept that not everything can be done perfectly\n   - Delegate when possible\n   - Say no to things that do not align with your priorities\n\n4. Protect Your Energy\n   - Identify your peak productivity hours and do deep work then\n   - Batch similar tasks together\n   - Take real breaks (step away from screens)\n   - Schedule buffer time between meetings\n\n5. Invest in Recovery\n   - Weekends should include genuine rest, not just a different kind of work\n   - Plan vacations and take them without guilt\n   - Have at least one screen-free hour daily\n   - Engage in hobbies that have nothing to do with work\n\n6. Address the Root Causes\n   - If your workload is unsustainable, have an honest conversation with your manager\n   - If perfectionism drives overwork, consider therapy\n   - If financial fears keep you working excessively, create a financial plan\n\nSigns You Need to Rebalance:\n- Relationships are suffering\n- You feel guilty when not working\n- Physical health is declining\n- You cannot remember the last time you truly relaxed\n- You have lost interest in things you used to enjoy"
            ],
        ];
